{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["<b>And if any animal dies.</b> It would have been fitting to say וכי תמות, but had it said so, it would imply that the carcass (nevelah) imparts impurity through contact only when it is whole and entire, whereas a part of it would not impart impurity. But when it says וכי ימות, this indicates that even a part of it imparts impurity through contact. Therefore it used וכי ימות מן הבהמה in the masculine form — and it is like the expression וכי יפול מנבלתם, for nevelah is indeed a feminine noun, yet it used יפול in the masculine form to refer to a part of it. And the Sages of blessed memory expounded ״הנוגע בנבלתה״ and ״האוכל מנבלתה״ — to exclude the horns and the hooves and the skin and the hair and the bones. And so we find in the case of Israel, who brought many leather utensils from the camp of Midian, and Scripture rendered them fit by sprinkling upon them the waters of the nun (purification waters); for if the skin of a carcass imparted impurity like the flesh of the carcass itself, no act of rendering fit would have availed them, since a carcass has no process of rendering fit. And so we find with Solomon, peace be upon him, that he made a throne of bones, as it is written (I Kings 10:18): ״ויעש המלך כסא שן גדול״ — and this is the bone of the elephant."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:24:54.393378571Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}